+#!/usr/bin/env bash
+# Run one command, preserving its exact argv, bounded combined output, and status.
+set -u
+
+if [ "$#" -lt 2 ]; then
+  echo "usage: run_logged.sh LOG COMMAND [ARG ...]" >&2
+  exit 2
+fi
+
+log_path="$1"
+shift
+mkdir -p "$(dirname "$log_path")"
+{
+  printf 'COMMAND:'
+  printf ' %q' "$@"
+  printf '\n'
+  "$@"
+  command_status=$?
+  printf 'EXIT_STATUS: %s\n' "$command_status"
+  exit "$command_status"
+} >"$log_path" 2>&1

codex
The clean LLVM definition executes all reviewer-selected boundaries to `.K` with the expected semantic strings and clean control cells. The Haskell definition also compiles from source, and the combined three-claim proof plus both loop claims close independently with exit 0 and `#Top`. The entry claim is taking longer when selected alone; I’m letting that run finish because it is the strongest check that the helper claims are available as actual lemmas rather than an aggregate-output artifact.
